{"File":"/home/prem/Projects/Arch Drop/ECOSYSTEM_RESEARCH_REPORT.md","LineNumber":23,"LineContent":"   Monolithic cross-device tools fail because they tightly couple discovery, physical transport, NAT traversal, and application protocols into a single layer. KDE Connect's hardcoded reliance on local LAN UDP broadcast (`port 1716`) renders it inoperable across cellular networks, split subnets, or public Wi-Fi client isolation without static IP hacks. Decoupling the application layer from the transport layer via a lightweight peer-to-peer WireGuard mesh overlay (e.g. Tailscale/DERP or NetBird ICE/STUN/TURN) provides secure, transparent, zero-configuration cross-network communication."}
{"File":"/home/prem/Projects/Arch Drop/ECOSYSTEM_RESEARCH_REPORT.md","LineNumber":200,"LineContent":"  * Relay Plane: DERP (Designated Encrypted Relay for Packets) over HTTPS/WebSockets (TCP port `443`)."}
{"File":"/home/prem/Projects/Arch Drop/ECOSYSTEM_RESEARCH_REPORT.md","LineNumber":218,"LineContent":"  * 100% compatible with upstream Tailscale client data planes: WireGuard UDP, DERP relays, STUN. Control plane uses gRPC and HTTP REST over TLS."}
{"File":"/home/prem/Projects/Arch Drop/ECOSYSTEM_RESEARCH_REPORT.md","LineNumber":225,"LineContent":"  * *Weaknesses:* Requires hosting an external VPS with open public ports to act as coordination and DERP relay server."}
